1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:07,119
This is RC in a flying sprint: top speed through a ten-yard zone, filmed in super slow motion.

2
00:00:07,119 --> 00:00:15,689
Everything you see is eight times slower than real life, and the lines on his body are computer tracking of his joints.

3
00:00:16,583 --> 00:00:21,339
His flying sprint speed was nineteen to almost twenty miles an hour.

4
00:00:21,339 --> 00:00:31,410
His goal is twenty-one feet nine inches, and by his own speed equation that needs about nineteen point six miles an hour in the approach itself.

5
00:00:31,410 --> 00:00:38,194
His approach runs averaged about eighteen point seven, so he needs roughly one more mile an hour.

6
00:00:39,083 --> 00:00:43,504
Now watch his right leg, in red, while the foot is on the ground.

7
00:00:43,504 --> 00:00:50,849
His ground contact time is about a tenth of a second, and the knee bends only a few degrees after touchdown.

8
00:00:50,849 --> 00:00:54,454
That short, stiff contact is what elite sprinters do.

9
00:00:54,454 --> 00:00:54,998
Keep it.

10
00:00:55,850 --> 00:01:01,073
His step rate, about four and a half steps per second, is close to elite sprinters.

11
00:01:01,073 --> 00:01:07,933
His step length is what's short: about two leg lengths per step, where elite men are close to two and a half.

12
00:01:07,933 --> 00:01:17,183
A longer step comes from more force into the ground, so that's a job for the weight room, Romanian deadlifts and broad jumps, not a change in form.

13
00:01:18,083 --> 00:01:23,821
Here's the main thing to change: touchdown distance, how far in front of his hips the foot lands.

14
00:01:23,821 --> 00:01:31,452
Frame by frame into touchdown, the foot lands about fifteen inches ahead of his hips, still moving forward, which causes braking.

15
00:01:31,452 --> 00:01:36,243
Faster sprinters land closer under the body, with the foot already sweeping back.

16
00:01:36,243 --> 00:01:38,964
Cue: step down and back, land under your hips.

17
00:01:38,964 --> 00:01:45,411
Drills: A-skips into straight-leg bounds, and wicket runs, with mini-hurdles about six and a half feet apart.

18
00:01:46,283 --> 00:01:56,250
In the approach-run simulations, his step length stays the same, but his flight time, the time in the air each step, is about twenty milliseconds longer.

19
00:01:56,250 --> 00:02:01,135
So his step rate drops from four and a half to about four steps per second.

20
00:02:01,135 --> 00:02:04,327
Try keeping the same quick rhythm and just relax.

21
00:02:04,327 --> 00:02:08,235
We only have a few steps of this, so it's something to test.

22
00:02:09,150 --> 00:02:13,866
His arms stay in time with his legs, but the arm swing is lopsided.

23
00:02:13,866 --> 00:02:18,793
It goes well behind him, but in front the hand only reaches mid-chest.

24
00:02:18,793 --> 00:02:23,860
Research says the arms mainly balance the legs, so this is low priority.

25
00:02:23,860 --> 00:02:29,139
One thing to test: hip to lip, hands from the hip pocket up to chin height.

26
00:02:30,050 --> 00:02:34,196
His right and left legs look the same, including his takeoff leg.

27
00:02:34,196 --> 00:02:42,743
Next session we'll film again, add jumps into the pit, and check touchdown distance, step length, and approach speed against the goal.
